-- title:
--   Gluing a Cartier datum into an invertible ideal sheaf
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a scheme and $\iota$ an index type (both in the same universe). Given a family $U : \iota \to X.\mathrm{affineOpens}$ of affine open subschemes whose underlying opens satisfy $\bigsqcup_i U_i = \top$, i.e. they cover $X$, and sections $g_i \in \Gamma(X, U_i)$ for each $i$, assume: (i) for every $i$ and every affine open $W$ with $W \le U_i$, the restriction of $g_i$ along the inclusion is a non-zero-divisor in $\Gamma(X, W)$; (ii) for all $i, j$ and every affine open $W$ contained in both $U_i$ and $U_j$, the principal ideals $\Gamma(X,W)\cdot (g_i|_W)$ and $\Gamma(X,W)\cdot (g_j|_W)$ coincide. Then there exists an ideal sheaf datum $I : X.\mathrm{IdealSheafData}$ such that, first, $I$ is invertible in the sense of the project's predicate `IsInvertible`: for every point $x \in X$ there are an affine open $U$, a section $f \in \Gamma(X,U)$ with $x \in X.\mathrm{basicOpen}\, f$, and a non-zero-divisor $g \in \Gamma(X, X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\, f)$ with $I$'s ideal at the affine open $X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\, f$ equal to $\mathrm{span}\{g\}$; and second, for every $i$ and every affine open $W \le U_i$, the ideal $I.\mathrm{ideal}\, W$ of $\Gamma(X,W)$ is the principal ideal generated by $g_i|_W$.
--
--   This is the passage from a Cartier datum $\{(U_i, g_i)\}$, given by local equations that are non-zero-divisors and agree up to units on overlaps, to the associated invertible ideal sheaf $\mathcal{O}_X(-D)$ of the effective Cartier divisor $D$, with its prescribed sections on affine opens. It is the entry point for twisting a model by a divisor given by local equations, and is used in the construction of presentations of the models of modular curves at $p$ appearing later in the development.
